1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,106
Lesson 5.5.

2
00:00:02,486 --> 00:00:04,015
Other physical substrates.

3
00:00:05,117 --> 00:00:07,855
Silicon pbits and coupled oscillators are

4
00:00:07,855 --> 00:00:10,527
not the only ways to compute with noise.

5
00:00:10,907 --> 00:00:14,667
There are circuits where information
is carried by particles that

6
00:00:14,667 --> 00:00:18,660
diffuse at random, superconducting
neurons, networks of resistors and

7
00:00:18,660 --> 00:00:21,900
capacitors that diffuse
voltage, and chemical reactions.

8
00:00:22,280 --> 00:00:26,777
In this lesson we tour that zoo
and what each substrate brings.

9
00:00:27,883 --> 00:00:31,954
In a Brownian circuit, a token,
for example an isolated electron

10
00:00:31,954 --> 00:00:35,516
or a magnetic skyrmion,
diffuses at random along a wire.

11
00:00:35,896 --> 00:00:38,244
Three pieces suffice to build logic.

12
00:00:38,624 --> 00:00:42,262
A Hub, where the token can exit
through any of three wires.

13
00:00:42,642 --> 00:00:45,545
A CJoin, which only
lets tokens through when

14
00:00:45,545 --> 00:00:48,117
two arrive together, one on each input.

15
00:00:48,497 --> 00:00:51,331
And a Ratchet, which
prevents going backward.

16
00:00:51,711 --> 00:00:54,165
With them one builds any circuit, and the

17
00:00:54,165 --> 00:00:57,517
computation advances by itself,
driven by thermal noise.

18
00:00:58,650 --> 00:00:59,435
How long does it take?

19
00:00:59,815 --> 00:01:04,160
Without ratchets, a token crossing
twenty sites by free diffusion takes

20
00:01:04,160 --> 00:01:08,505
on average about two hundred ten time
units, with huge variability: the

21
00:01:08,505 --> 00:01:11,809
standard deviation is
eighty-four percent of the mean.

22
00:01:12,189 --> 00:01:16,397
With a bias that doubles the forward
rate, the time drops to about

23
00:01:16,397 --> 00:01:20,159
nineteen units and the variability
to thirty-eight percent.

24
00:01:20,539 --> 00:01:25,270
The price is dissipating log
two, in k_B T, per step: exactly

25
00:01:25,270 --> 00:01:29,381
the trade-off of thermodynamic
uncertainty relations.

26
00:01:30,483 --> 00:01:36,638
An rf-SQUID, a superconducting loop with
a Josephson junction, has a double-well

27
00:01:36,638 --> 00:01:42,176
potential in magnetic flux, with
barrier and tilt tunable by bias lines.

28
00:01:42,556 --> 00:01:45,981
At millikelvin temperatures,
thermal noise makes it

29
00:01:45,981 --> 00:01:49,204
hop between wells: it is
a thermodynamic neuron.

30
00:01:49,584 --> 00:01:54,255
Its advantage is speed and low
dissipation; its cost, cryogenics.

31
00:01:54,635 --> 00:01:57,527
Below a certain temperature, hopping stops

32
00:01:57,527 --> 00:02:00,556
being thermal and becomes
quantum tunneling.

33
00:02:00,936 --> 00:02:06,165
Microscopic mechanical oscillators have
also been proposed as Langevin devices.

34
00:02:07,283 --> 00:02:12,174
A network of resistors and
capacitors satisfies C V̇ = −L V,

35
00:02:12,174 --> 00:02:16,982
where L is the graph Laplacian:
voltage diffuses like heat.

36
00:02:17,362 --> 00:02:21,514
If the graph is the Cayley graph
of a group, that diffusion computes

37
00:02:21,514 --> 00:02:25,727
the return probability of a random
walk, and it is the physical basis

38
00:02:25,727 --> 00:02:29,024
of the factoring-by-diffusion
algorithm of unit seven.

39
00:02:29,404 --> 00:02:33,147
Chemical reaction networks,
for their part, compute with

40
00:02:33,147 --> 00:02:37,824
concentrations and are analyzed with
the circuit theory of lesson 3.9.

41
00:02:38,950 --> 00:02:39,760
Let's compare.

42
00:02:40,140 --> 00:02:45,216
CMOS and magnetic pbits work at
room temperature and in nanoseconds.

43
00:02:45,596 --> 00:02:48,820
Coupled oscillators solve
continuous problems.

44
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:52,977
Brownian circuits compute
without a clock, but slowly.

45
00:02:53,357 --> 00:02:56,566
SQUIDs are fast, but need cryogenics.

46
00:02:56,946 --> 00:03:00,065
RC networks diffuse
without useful noise, and

47
00:03:00,065 --> 00:03:03,599
chemical reactions are slow
but massively parallel.

48
00:03:03,979 --> 00:03:05,770
No substrate wins at everything.

49
00:03:06,883 --> 00:03:11,403
In the lab seven challenge you will
simulate a Brownian adder with Gillespie.

50
00:03:11,783 --> 00:03:15,925
In unit seven we will use RC
networks for order finding.

51
00:03:16,305 --> 00:03:22,292
And the conductance theory of lesson
3.9 serves to design chemical computers.

52
00:03:23,417 --> 00:03:27,946
Common mistakes: believing clockless
Brownian computation is free.

53
00:03:28,326 --> 00:03:32,291
Ignoring the cooling power
of cryogenic devices.

54
00:03:32,671 --> 00:03:37,461
Confusing the deterministic diffusion of
an RC network with stochastic sampling.

55
00:03:37,841 --> 00:03:40,951
And comparing substrates without
fixing the task and the precision.

56
00:03:42,083 --> 00:03:45,478
In summary: Brownian circuits
compute with diffusing

57
00:03:45,478 --> 00:03:48,612
tokens and pay for their
speed with dissipation.

58
00:03:48,992 --> 00:03:54,094
SQUIDs are cryogenic thermodynamic
neurons, RC networks diffuse voltage

59
00:03:54,094 --> 00:03:58,765
over a graph, and chemical reactions
compute with concentrations.

60
00:03:59,145 --> 00:04:02,307
Each substrate has its own
primitive and its own limit.

61
00:04:03,417 --> 00:04:07,606
To practice: compute the
first-passage time of a free walk.

62
00:04:07,986 --> 00:04:11,361
Compute the entropy dissipated
per step with a bias of two.

63
00:04:11,741 --> 00:04:13,889
Explain why ratchets are needed.

64
00:04:14,269 --> 00:04:16,756
And write the dynamics of an RC network.
